research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-topology-uniform.log-8393-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-topology-uniform.log-8394-Filter convergence and ultrafilter compactness from [[nets-and-filters]] describe convergence without numerical distance, and the diagonal characterization from [[hausdorff-via-the-diagonal]] supplies the separation condition used here. The group laws and inverse laws of [[monoids-groups-and-subgroups]] underlie the translation-invariant examples. These prerequisites let a relation of nearness between pairs govern topology, continuity, Cauchy behaviour, and completion.
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-topology-uniform.log-8395-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-topology-uniform.log-8396-The development defines uniformities by entourages, uniform covers, and gauges, proving the entourage-cover dictionary in ZF and stating dependent choice precisely for the gauge construction. It then develops induced topology, separatedness, uniform continuity, Cauchy filters, the complete Hausdorff reflection, and compactness through total boundedness. Countable bases and continuous pseudometrics lead to uniformizability results, while left, right, upper, Roelcke, pointwise, and uniform-convergence structures provide further uniformities.
--
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-topology-uniform.log-9052-2.1 For each $p\in\mathcal P$ and each positive radius, its ball is an original entourage by step 1.1. Hence every finite intersection defining a basic entourage of the gauge belongs to $\mathcal U$. [step 1.1, L3]
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-topology-uniform.log-9053-
research/audit/wave7-dispatch/audit-beta-topology-uniform.log-9054-2.2 Conversely every original entourage $E$ contains a positive-radius ball for at least one $p\in\mathcal P$ by step 1.1, so it belongs to the gauge uniformity. [step 1.1, L3]
